#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Fake `claude` CLI: a deterministic CI fixture for the e2e suite. It is NOT part of the product.

CI has no Claude account, so `run_acceptance.py --ai-stub` puts this script where the real agent looks for
`claude` and the real request-service / agent code runs unchanged against it. It speaks only the subset the agent uses:

  claude --version
  claude --help
  claude auth status --json                         loggedIn follows FAKE_CLAUDE_LOGGED_IN (exit 1 when false, like the real CLI)
  claude -p --output-format json --tools "" [--no-session-persistence] --system-prompt <text> [--model <m>]   prompt on stdin (ai.complete)
  claude --print [--output-format stream-json --verbose] [--permission-mode plan --tools Read,Glob,Grep]
                                                    prompt on stdin (agent.execPrompt, read-only analysis; since CR-REQ-042). The old form
                                                    `claude --print <prompt> ...` (ORCA_AGENT_PROMPT_VIA_ARGV=1) is still accepted. stdout is plain
                                                    text, or with stream-json one JSON event per line (system init, assistant text, result), like the real CLI

Answers are keyed on the prompts the real request-service builds (classification, Solution, Plan, and the read-only
diagnosis / findings / answer analyses) and are written to pass the real parsers and validators (ParseClassificationProposal,
SolutionOptions.Validate + coverage, ParsePlanOutput, ValidatePlanProposal, ValidateByKind). A prompt it does not recognise fails loudly instead of inventing an answer.

Execution (agent.execPrompt with accessMode=write: `claude --print --output-format stream-json --verbose --dangerously-skip-permissions`, cwd the task's
worktree): a task that has a spec is a contract run, whose prompt asks for a result block; the answer is a valid `done` block between the nonce markers
the prompt gives. Any other task (the default opener "Complete the following task.") gets one plain line. The fixture changes no file, claims none in
files_changed, and lists a passing check only when the prompt requires one (marked as fake). Read-only prompts are refused when the bypass flag is present.

Language: a line `Output language: Vietnamese.` before <request> (the directive request-service adds, CR-REQ-046) makes the human-readable strings of the
Solution, Plan and read-only answers Vietnamese (table VI); ids, enums, labels and keys never change. Every call records `outputLanguage` (vi, en or "").

A reviewer's change request (a <feedback> block after </request>) is echoed in the answer, so each round differs (a revised Plan counts its round
from the marker in the <prior_plan> block); the code [stub:fail-once] in that feedback makes the first answer fail and later ones succeed, and
[stub:slow] delays the answer (FAKE_CLAUDE_SLOW_SECONDS, default 6). Both apply to Solutions and to Plans.

A Plan that nobody asked to revise (the automatic run of CR-REQ-044 has no <feedback>) is failed on demand with the file `fail_plan_next` in the state
dir: its content `<count>:<kind>` makes the next <count> Plan prompts fail (`error` = the call fails, `invalid` = the reply is not JSON) or run
slowly (`slow` = answer normally after FAKE_CLAUDE_SLOW_SECONDS, so a test can act while the run is in flight); each prompt decrements the count,
and an empty or exhausted file does nothing. A native Plan gets one repair attempt, so `invalid` needs a count of 2 to fail a run.

State (calls.jsonl, logged_in, fail-once markers, fail_plan_next) lives in $FAKE_CLAUDE_STATE_DIR, default ~/.fake-claude. The agent strips FAKE_* variables
from the environment of `claude -p`, so the login state can also be switched with the `logged_in` file (0 or 1).
POSIX only (macOS, Linux); a Windows agent resolves claude.cmd, so the stub does not apply there.
"""
